# SABOTAGE: the CROSS-NODE INSERT-TIME EXACTLY-ONCE declaration of the L4 multi-node Oban proof
# (`Samen.MultiNode.UniqueWorker`'s `unique: [keys: [:key], …]`). This is the tier my earlier
# multinode report flagged as unfalsifiable: the proof has THREE named tests, but nothing flipped
# any of them, so its green line was asserted-by-inspection rather than refutable. This patch makes
# the dedup half of that line refutable, and the proof's own `MUST_FAIL` now catches it.
#
# WHAT BREAKS. `unique` is Oban's insert-time exactly-once primitive: enqueuing the SAME logical job
# (same `"key"` args) any number of times, from EITHER node, collapses to ONE `oban_jobs` row, so
# the side effect fires ONCE cluster-wide. Dropping the declaration does not change the crash story
# or the fetch path — it silently turns "fan out this job from both nodes" into one execution PER
# ENQUEUE (the two-node proof fans it out 40× from A and 40× from B, i.e. 80 executions of one
# logical job). That is the real regression shape this declaration exists to prevent, and it is a
# one-line deletion: the kind of edit that looks tidy in review and doubles a customer's side
# effects in production.
#
# WHY THIS AND NOT THE "exactly-once: 120 distinct jobs" TEST. That test's guarantee is
# `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` inside Oban's engine, coordinated by Postgres — no file in this
# repository supplies it, so no in-repo patch can break it, and a patch that faked a double-grab
# would be sabotaging the detector rather than the guarantee. The tier's exactly-once claim is
# therefore asserted at two levels: the FETCH level (dependency-enforced, not reachable from here)
# and the INSERT-time DEDUP level (this declaration — reachable, and until now unrefuted). This
# patch refutes the level a repository change can actually break, and says so rather than implying
# the whole tier is now adversarially covered.
#
# RE-ANCHORED (2026-10-23) — so the patch certifies on EVERY host. The anchor used to be the
# two-node test alone (`test/multinode/oban_multinode_test.exs`, `ENV: SAMEN_MULTINODE=1`), whose
# `setup_all` needs `epmd` and a working Erlang distribution. On a host where distribution cannot
# start (`could not start distribution (is epmd up?)`) `setup_all` invalidates all three tests and
# prints no `N) test …` header, so the replay failed closed with "no test-failure headers found" —
# the sabotage was uncertifiable there through no fault of the declaration. The anchor is now the
# epmd-FREE leg of the same guarantee, `test/oban_unique_insert_dedup_test.exs`: the identical
# `Oban.insert/1` fan-out (40×, the same order as one node of the two-node proof) counted in
# `oban_jobs` against the sandboxed test repo, with `NoUniqueWorker` as the in-run negative
# control. `ENV:` is no longer needed (that file is not ExUnit-excluded). The cross-node leg is
# unchanged and still runs wherever a distribution is available.
#
# WHAT THE NAMED TEST SHOWS. With the declaration gone, the single-node fan-out lands 40 `oban_jobs`
# rows instead of 1, so `job_rows(UniqueWorker, key) == 1` reads 40. The assertion is the only thing
# that moves: the negative control (`NoUniqueWorker`, no `unique`, same body) still lands one row
# per enqueue and still asserts `== 40`, so the failure is the exactly-once claim being false — not
# a harness boot failure, not a setup error, and not a tautology.
#
# NOT TOUCHED, deliberately: the negative control (`NoUniqueWorker`), the other two proof workers,
# the `mn_exec` ledger and every detector that reads it, the proof's own enqueue seam
# (`Harness.insert_each_on/2` — the path that DOES honour `unique`), the two-node topology, and the
# database both peers share. The sabotage removes ONE worker option and nothing else.
#
# The deletion also collapses the call's trailing comma: dropping the last argument of a multi-line
# call without re-terminating the new last line is a SYNTAX ERROR, not a flip — the first cut of this
# patch did exactly that and the harness refused it ("suite failed but no test-failure headers found
# (compile error?)"), which is the fail-closed behaviour a sabotage harness needs. Two lines change,
# and the result reads exactly like the sibling workers' one-line form. No warning is introduced (the
# `Oban.Job.states()` reference the option held leaves nothing unused behind), so nothing needs
# confining for a `--warnings-as-errors` compile.
# APP: samen_core
# TEST_FILES: test/oban_unique_insert_dedup_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: uniqueness dedup: the unique job fanned out 40x from ONE node lands ONE row
--- a/samen_core/test/support/multinode/workers.ex
+++ b/samen_core/test/support/multinode/workers.ex
@@ -34,8 +34,7 @@ defmodule Samen.MultiNode.UniqueWorker do
   """
   use Oban.Worker,
     queue: :default,
-    max_attempts: 1,
-    unique: [keys: [:key], period: 300, states: Oban.Job.states()]
+    max_attempts: 1
 
   @impl Oban.Worker
   def perform(%Oban.Job{args: %{"key" => key}}) do
